Drives its own private GC state (its own `gcBindMemory`/`gcInit`-bound arena),
scripting a prime/reclaim/differential sequence against that private
collector, not the ambient runtime one. Its assertions are on that private
collector's own span-presence cache behavior under reclaim
(`idxSpanPresent`/`idxRemoveSpan`, `runtime/gc/gcindex.bit`) — correct
regardless of whether the ambient (libbitrt) collector ever runs.

Every allocation this program makes goes through the private `g` handle, so
the ambient collector never traces or sweeps any of its objects. This
program's own backing arrays (`arena`/`indexArena`/`heapBlock`/`heapLock`/
`lock`/`state`/`roots`/`bodies`/`meta`) are read once more after
`gcBindMemory`/`gcInit`, at the trailing `rooted` check, for the same reason
`gcindexchurn`'s and `gcslotbitmap`'s own leading comments give (#4097/#4184):
this compiler's precise stack maps are interval-based, so a binding never read
again by name can be swept out from under a still-running function.
